      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.1.04-15",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Sampling bias and random variation differ. A chance-selected small sample can give a variable result without deliberate bias, while a consistently convenient frame can omit the same group repeatedly. A remedy should match the problem. More chance-selected observations can improve precision or coverage, but changing the frame may be necessary to include missing locations or people.",
